<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wichmann von Seeburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1116–<a href="/years/1192">1192</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wichmann von Seeburg was archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> from 1152 until his death in 1192. A leading prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, he supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> and helped negotiate the Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> in 1177. He promoted German settlement east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and around 1188 granted Magdeburg the town law that became a model for many towns of central and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Wichmann was the second son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> count Gero of Seeburg, who died in 1122, and of Matilda, a daughter of the Wettin count Thimo the Brave. He studied theology at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-paris">University of Paris</a> and became a canon at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halberstadt">Halberstadt</a>. He was elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Naumburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeitz">Zeitz</a> in 1149, was ordained the next year, and made large donations to the monasteries of Pforta and Zeitz.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>A regular guest at the court of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-iii-of-germany">Conrad III of Germany</a>, he was appointed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> by Conrad's nephew, the newly crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>, in 1152, and had to overcome the resistance of Pope Eugene III and his successor Anastasius IV. He worked to extend the archdiocese and its economy and promoted trade in its towns. In 1157 he joined the Ascanian margrave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-the-bear">Albert the Bear</a> to retake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> and Jüterbog, settling Flemish colonists around them, and in 1170 he endowed the new Cistercian monastery of Zinna.</p><p>Wichmann backed Barbarossa against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-iii">Pope Alexander III</a>, attending the Council of Pavia in 1160 and supporting the antipopes Victor IV and Paschal III before trying to mediate. On a pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> in 1164 he fell for a time into Turkish hands. He joined the princes' war against Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a> in 1166 and saw his lands devastated, and after the peace talks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1177 he worked for Henry's deposition and ban in 1180, after which he became the principal secular authority in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1180 he became the first prince-archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>, and in 1180 and 1185 he gave parts of his possessions to Seitenstetten Abbey in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and to the Bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passau">Passau</a>. He died near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kothen">Köthen</a> on 25 August 1192 and was buried in his own cathedral. The city and diocese of Magdeburg flourished under him, and he is credited with the first codification of Magdeburg town law in 1188.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wichmann von Seeburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Archbishop <b>Wichmann</b> granted Magdeburg its town law around 1188, a model copied across central and eastern Europe.</blockquote><a href="/state/archbishopric-of-magdeburg">Archbishopric of Magdeburg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Sonntagberg, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in Könnern, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48,14.76&amp;year=1165" aria-label="Sonntagberg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Wichmann von Seeburg"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5836 5925h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5836 5925h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48,14.76&amp;year=1165">What was here in 1165? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
